{
  "id": "find-k-pairs-with-smallest-sums",
  "questionId": "find-k-pairs-with-smallest-sums",
  "slug": "find-k-pairs-with-smallest-sums",
  "title": "Find K Pairs with Smallest Sums",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Heap",
  "patterns": [
    "heap",
    "min-heap",
    "k-way merge"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "generating all n*m pairs is wasteful when k is much smaller than n*m - most pairs will never be needed",
    "think of it as merging n sorted lists, where list i is nums1[i] paired with every value in nums2 in order - each such list is already sorted by sum since nums2 is sorted",
    "seed a min-heap with the first pair (index j=0) from each of these n lists, capped at k initial entries since you'll never need more than k lists open at once",
    "pop the smallest sum k times; each time you pop a pair (i, j), push the next pair (i, j+1) from that same list if it exists. the k pops in order are your answer"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "materializing the full cross product of nums1 and nums2 before sorting, which wastes memory and time when k is small",
    "seeding the heap with all n starting pairs even when k is smaller than n, doing unnecessary heap work",
    "forgetting to stop advancing a list's pointer once its nums2 index runs out, causing an out-of-bounds access"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given two lists of integers, `nums1` and `nums2`, each sorted in\nascending order, and a number `k`. A pair is formed by taking one value\nfrom `nums1` and one from `nums2`, written `[nums1[i], nums2[j]]`, and\nevery possible combination of indices is a valid pair.\n\n**Return the `k` pairs with the smallest sums. If there are fewer than `k` possible pairs in total, return all of them.**\n\nOrder the result by sum ascending; break ties by the `nums1` value ascending, and if that also ties, by the `nums2` value ascending, so the output is fully deterministic.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums1.length, nums2.length <= 1000",
      "-10^9 <= nums1[i], nums2[i] <= 10^9",
      "nums1 and nums2 are each sorted in ascending order",
      "1 <= k <= 10^4"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "k larger than the total number of possible pairs",
      "duplicate values within nums1 or nums2",
      "either list has only one element",
      "k = 1, wanting just the single smallest-sum pair"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums1 = [1, 7, 11], nums2 = [2, 4, 6], k = 3",
        "output": "[[1, 2], [1, 4], [1, 6]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1, 2], [1, 4], [1, 6]]",
        "explanation": "the smallest sums come from pairing 1 with each of 2, 4, 6 (sums 3, 5, 7), all smaller than any pair using 7 or 11"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums1 = [1, 1, 2], nums2 = [1, 2, 3], k = 2",
        "output": "[[1, 1], [1, 1]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1, 1], [1, 1]]",
        "explanation": "both 1s in nums1 paired with the 1 in nums2 give sum 2, the smallest possible, so those two pairs are returned"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums1 = [1, 2], nums2 = [3], k = 3",
        "output": "[[1, 3], [2, 3]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1, 3], [2, 3]]",
        "explanation": "only 2 pairs exist in total (fewer than k = 3), so both are returned"
      }
